# Deployment

Odroe builds product artifacts; the platform configuration remains owned by
your application.

## Static web

```sh
dart run odroe build --no-server web
```

For a Flutter WebAssembly client, pass Flutter's target and flag after `--`:

```sh
dart run odroe build --no-server -- web --wasm
```

JavaScript and WebAssembly builds use the same browser DOM path for Document
handoff, streamed frames, semantic HTML transition, and external Router
navigation.

Document routes are rendered through the real server. Pure document routes
produce semantic HTML; Flutter routes also keep their bootstrap and handoff
state. Files from `public/` are copied without a bundler.

Odroe-managed Flutter Web output must resolve inside the project's `build/`
tree. The CLI rewrites Flutter's `--output` to a clean hidden sibling during the
build, then atomically swaps each requested directory as part of one grouped
publication. If a later Web target or the final Native validation fails, Odroe
restores every earlier directory before returning the failure. Removed routes
and assets therefore do not survive from a previous successful build. Source
directories, `public/`, and absolute external outputs are never managed
replacement roots.

Static file routes and locations returned from `lib/prerender.dart` are the
default source of truth. Link discovery is opt-in with `--prerender-crawl`.
Builds use 4 concurrent requests and stop at 1000 routes or 1 MiB of HTML per
route unless `--prerender-concurrency`, `--prerender-max-routes`, or
`--prerender-max-response-bytes` changes that budget.
The optional crawler follows same-origin, query-free links whose paths are
extensionless or end in `.html`.
Document-only builds assemble in a sibling staging directory and replace the
previous static output only after every route succeeds.
The prerender server mounts no static root while generating pages, so neither
an old build nor a same-path HTML file from `public/` can replace the current
route response.

When a full-stack deployment also needs the default Native server, its
`odroe.yaml` selection makes `dart run odroe build web` include the SQLite
history, Flutter client, and prerendered routes in one deployable bundle. A
Native product without SQLite migrations omits that config. For a
document-only product such as this website, no Flutter target is required:

```sh
dart run odroe build --no-server
```

`--no-server` still starts the generated Dart server from source for
prerendering; it only skips the deployable server artifact. Omit it when the
deployment also needs the default native server.

## Native server

```sh
dart run odroe build --server-only
```

The result is a native bundle rather than an isolated executable:

```text
build/odroe/server/
├── .odroe-native-bundle
├── bin/
│   └── server
├── lib/
└── migrations/  # when SQLite history is selected
    ├── 0001_posts.sql
    └── 0002_unify_posts.sql
```

Windows names the executable `bin/server.exe`; the bundle root and the rest of
the layout stay the same.

A full-stack build uses the same root and adds the complete Web application:

```sh
dart run odroe build web
```

```text
build/odroe/server/
├── bin/
├── lib/
├── migrations/  # when selected
└── build/
    └── web/
        ├── flutter_bootstrap.js
        ├── main.dart.js
        └── posts/42/index.html  # example prerendered route
```

This is the deployment boundary: move the one bundle, not a server directory
plus an unrelated static build.

Migration files are copied byte-for-byte through a sibling staging directory;
the AOT server remains staged while Flutter and prerender run. Odroe layers the
actual Flutter output and `--prerender-output` into `<bundle>/build/web`, checks
the migration source again, and replaces the previous root once. A failed
Flutter build, prerender, Web copy, or migration recheck leaves the previous
server and client together. `--server-only` intentionally publishes no
`build/web`, including when it replaces an earlier full-stack bundle, so stale
client code cannot masquerade as the current release. Nested Flutter/prerender
roots and symbolic links inside either Web tree fail closed instead of copying
an ambiguous or external artifact. A
hidden ownership marker keeps the CLI from overwriting an unrelated directory.
One project build lock covers route generation, AOT and Flutter compilation,
prerendering, Web assembly, migration validation, and publication, so concurrent
Odroe build commands cannot cross-pair one server with another client's output.
The generated bootstrap also uses that marker to identify an owned bundle. It
derives the root from the resolved executable and changes the runtime working
directory before application creation, so the entry may be invoked from any
caller working directory. A compiled entry requires an exact, regular marker
file; a missing marker, directory, symbolic link, or changed content fails
before application state is opened. Non-product source and development runs
keep the project working directory and do not require a bundle marker.
CLI-managed prerender explicitly retains the project working directory so
build-time source resources keep their source semantics, but only after the
compiled entry validates the marker. This does not weaken the deployed bundle
contract.

The full-stack starter selects `migrations` in the application-owned
`odroe.yaml`; `--sqlite-migrations <path>` can override one build. If neither
config nor CLI selects a history, Odroe never interprets a PostgreSQL or MySQL
application's generic migration directory as SQLite. It also refuses to replace
an existing bundle that would silently lose its selected history. Deploy the
whole root; copying only `bin/server` can omit required native libraries.

`ODROE_HOST` and `ODROE_PORT` configure
the listener; platforms that provide `PORT` are supported by the generated
bootstrap. In an owned bundle, static files default to `<bundle>/build/web`
regardless of the caller working directory. Set `ODROE_WEB_ROOT` to an explicit
directory, or to an empty string to disable the static root. `build web` already
places assets in the default location. A server-only image must add an explicit
static tree, set another path, or disable static serving.

The generated full-stack starter defaults SQLite to `.odroe/app.sqlite3`
relative to the effective runtime root. In an owned bundle that is the bundle
root; in source and development runs it remains the project process directory.
This default is local state, not durable production storage. Native production
must use one process and an explicit absolute path on a writable persistent
volume:

```sh
ODROE_SQLITE_PATH=/persistent/odroe/app.sqlite3 \
/app/server/bin/server
```

The self-located bundle root makes the default `migrations/` path resolve to the
validated history without a supervisor `cwd` setting. When history is mounted
elsewhere, override it explicitly:

```sh
ODROE_SQLITE_PATH=/persistent/odroe/app.sqlite3 \
ODROE_MIGRATIONS_PATH=/app/server/migrations \
/app/server/bin/server
```

The server validates the complete append-only history and applies pending files
before listening. Do not edit, remove, rename, or backfill an applied file;
publish a higher-numbered file. Back up the data volume before a production
migration. A failed file is rolled back, but an operational rollback still uses
your volume backup and application release process—there is no generated down
migration.

Do not share the starter's single SQLite connection across
multiple service replicas; use a server database such as PostgreSQL for
horizontal scaling.

Native static files are contained by their resolved public root on every
request. Every name uses `no-cache` with ETag and Last-Modified revalidation
instead of guessed immutable caching. Eligible text, JavaScript, JSON, SVG, and
Wasm files of at least 1 KiB stream with gzip when content encoding negotiation
selects it. Custom `IoServer.bind` callers can set
`compressStaticAssets: false` when an upstream proxy owns content encoding.
Exact files win. When no exact file exists, the request resolves to
`index.html` below that path. Generated indexes therefore serve route URLs
such as `/docs`, `/docs/`, and `/docs.v1` without running the dynamic
handler again for HTML-compatible requests. Explicit
`Accept: application/json` requests still reach the dynamic Document handoff;
responses vary by `Accept`.

The generated bootstrap creates the application `Server` once and forwards
both `handler` and `onError` to `IoServer.bind`, so native static, proxy,
metadata, and framing failures use the application reporter without
duplicating Server-owned handler or source-stream failures. Handwritten native
entrypoints should preserve the same ownership:

```dart
final appServer = await createServer();
final nativeServer = await IoServer.bind(
  appServer.handler,
  onError: appServer.onError,
);

// After the host stops accepting traffic:
await IoServer.close(nativeServer);
await appServer.close();
```

An IO callback starts only after the server-side response close attempt
settles, and its returned future is observed by a detached request task.
`HttpServer.close` does not await that task; `IoServer.close` does. Only after
the adapter drains should `Server.close` release process-owned pools and
telemetry through `onClose`. The generated bootstrap performs this order for
SIGINT on every platform and SIGTERM where supported. The first signal drains
in-flight requests gracefully; a second signal during that drain escalates to
a forced socket close. Binding failures also close the application. Keep
reporting bounded; delivery that must survive process shutdown belongs in an
application-owned durable queue.

The Native bundle is OS- and architecture-specific. Build on the target OS and
architecture, or in a compatible builder. For a Linux container, compile in a
Linux build stage. Do not copy a macOS bundle into the image, and do not detach
`bin/server` from its sibling `lib/` directory.

## Edge

Build the Cloudflare Workers target explicitly:

```sh
dart run odroe build --server-only --server-target cloudflare
```

For a Cloudflare Worker with Flutter Web and static HTML:

```sh
dart run odroe build --server-target cloudflare web
```

A Document-only Worker with static HTML does not need a Flutter target:

```sh
dart run odroe build --server-target cloudflare
```

If `wrangler.jsonc` declares an assets directory such as `build/web`, a
server-only Worker artifact does not create that directory. Build the assets
explicitly before local Wrangler or deployment validation, or remove the
assets binding when the application truly has no static output.

Odroe writes `build/odroe/cloudflare/server.js` and a thin `worker.mjs`.
Your application continues to own `wrangler.jsonc`; the build never overwrites
platform configuration. A non-server-only Cloudflare build runs the generated
Dart server source for prerendering instead of compiling a second temporary
Native bundle. An assets-only Flutter SSG instead uses
`build --no-server web` and deploys only `build/web`; a Document-only SSG can
omit the Flutter target.

The Fetch runtime has no reliable process-shutdown event, so its generated
bootstrap does not call `Server.close` or `onClose`. Durable bindings and D1
wrappers remain scoped to the host invocation instead of pretending to own an
isolate lifetime.

```json
{
  "$schema": "./node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "my-odroe-app",
  "main": "./build/odroe/cloudflare/worker.mjs",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["enable_request_signal"],
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "migrations_dir": "migrations"
    }
  ],
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./build/web",
    "html_handling": "drop-trailing-slash"
  }
}
```

When the Worker reads D1, keep schema changes in reviewable migration files and
apply them explicitly before local runtime verification. A project created by
`odroe init --full-stack` already owns a locked Wrangler toolchain. Run the safe
local path from that application root:

```sh
npm ci
dart run odroe build --no-server web
npm run cloudflare:migrate:local
npm run cloudflare:dev
```

Applications should own this toolchain instead of relying on an unversioned
global executable. Node is only a deployment-development dependency; it does
not enter the Dart application or Worker bundle.

The generated `cloudflare:dev` script calls
`odroe dev --server-target cloudflare --server-only`. Odroe compiles the current
Dart server into a temporary sibling, atomically replaces the server JavaScript
only after success, and passes that exact `worker.mjs` to local Wrangler. Source errors
in the initial generation or compile therefore exit before Wrangler starts.
After startup, source errors keep the last-known-good server JavaScript until
the next successful edit. The watcher neither rebuilds Flutter Web nor applies
D1 migrations; keep both explicit as shown.

The server entry should wrap `DB` through `D1SqlDatabase` in
`invocationModules`; it must not open SQLite or run asynchronous DDL during
Fetch bootstrap. Removing `--local` changes the remote database and therefore
belongs to the authorized deployment procedure, not the local smoke path.

`enable_request_signal` lets a real incoming client disconnect abort the Fetch
`Request.signal`, which completes `ServerRequest.cancelled`. A Node test can
abort a synthetic `Request` without proving this Cloudflare runtime
configuration.

The generated Fetch bootstrap creates the application `Server` once and
forwards both its invocation handler and reporter. Handwritten entrypoints
should preserve the same ownership. Unlike the native bootstrap, Fetch rejects
a `Future`-returning `createServer()` because the host export must be installed
synchronously; keep binding wrappers request-scoped instead of doing startup
I/O.

```dart
final appServer = createServer();
exportFetchHandler(
  appServer.invocationHandler,
  onError: appServer.onError,
);
```

The adapter reports its own status, header, `Response` construction, and
response-byte conversion failures once. Handler and source-stream failures
remain `Server`-owned. A bounded asynchronous reporter is registered through
the host `waitUntil` and does not delay the response.

`drop-trailing-slash` keeps generated directory indexes on canonical URLs such
as `/docs`. This hybrid configuration deliberately leaves
`not_found_handling` unset so browser navigation misses can reach dynamic
Document routes in the Worker.

For a fully prerendered SSG where every public navigation URL exists in
`build/web`, add `"not_found_handling": "404-page"` to serve the generated
`404.html`. With a compatibility date of `2025-04-01` or newer, Cloudflare
otherwise skips the Worker for navigation misses. Hybrid applications that
retain a static 404 can opt into `assets_navigation_has_no_effect` or scope
`run_worker_first` to their dynamic paths. See
[Cloudflare navigation routing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/routing/static-site-generation/#navigation-requests).

The target follows these rules:

- static assets remain asset-first to avoid unnecessary Worker invocations;
- the platform, rather than the Worker adapter, owns asset caching and encoding;
- dynamic routes use the standard Fetch `Request` and `Response` contract;
- D1 is passed as an explicit request binding;
- `waitUntil` tasks keep request modules alive until background work settles.

The required Node smoke covers streaming, synthetic cancellation, concurrency,
and the compiled artifact. Optional local Wrangler/Workerd and D1 integration
tests run when `ODROE_WRANGLER` is configured. This remains Preview support. No
remote Cloudflare deployment has been verified.
